

  <section xml:id="Math2301Day17">
    <title>A Very Spooky Theorem</title>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-review">
      <title>Review</title>
      <p>
        Answer the following questions:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Given a linear operator, what is the corresponding quadratic form?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Given an <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, what is an <m>n \times n</m> symmetric matrix that has the same quadratic form?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-statement">
      <title>The Statement</title>
      <p>
        We will prove the following strong version of the spectral theorem:
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-spectral">
        <title>Real spectral theorem</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>T</m> be a self-adjoint operator on a finite-dimensional real inner-product space <m>V</m>.
            Then <m>V</m> has an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors of <m>T</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      <!-- The spectral theorem guarantees a decomposition for the matrix stronger than regular diagonalization:

      <definition xml:id="def-orthogonal-diagonalizable">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A square matrix <m>Q</m> is <term>orthogonal</term> if <m>Q\trans Q=I</m>.
            A matrix <m>A</m> is <term>orthogonally diagonalizable</term> if <m>A=QD Q\trans</m> for an orthogonal <m>Q</m> and diagonal matrix <m>D</m>.
            In particular, <m>A</m> may be written as
            <me>
              A=\sum_i\lambda_i\vec{q}_i\vec{q}_i\trans
            </me>
            for orthonormal eigenvectors <m>q_i</m> is its <term>spectral decomposition</term>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>
      -->

      <p>
        The proof here is involved in part because this requires some tools from outside of linear algebra.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-optimization">
      <title>Optimization and Eigenvectors</title>
      <p>
        Recall from last class the quadratic form:
        <me>
          q_{T}(\vec{x})=\ip{\vec{x}}{T\vec{x}}
        </me>
        The argument is then built from the following intuition.
        Given two vectors of unit length, by the Cauchy<ndash/>Schwarz inequality, <m>\ip{\vec{u}}{\vec{v}}</m> is maximized when <m>\vec{u} = \vec{v}</m>.
        More generally, Cauchy<ndash/>Schwarz says that the inner product is maximized when two vectors are parallel.
        The quadratic form measures exactly the inner product between a vector <m>\vec{x}</m> and its image under <m>T</m>, <m>T \vec{x}</m>.
        Thus, it is reasonable to expect the quadratic form is maximized at some point when <m>\vec{x}</m> and <m>T\vec{x}</m> are parallel.
        That is precisely what it means to be an eigenvector.
        Thus, we can find an eigenvector by maximizing this quadratic form.
      </p>

      <p>
        For this let <m>\mathbb{S}^{d-1} = \{\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{d}: \norm{\vec{x}} = 1\}</m>.
      </p>

      <lemma xml:id="lem-maximizer-eigenvector">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>T: V \to V</m> be a self-adjoint operator on an inner product space <m>V</m>.
            Let <m>q_{T}</m> denote its associated quadratic form.
            Let <m>\vec{v}</m> be a maximizer of <m>q_{T}(\vec{x})</m> such that <m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{S}^{d-1}</m>.
            Then <m>\vec{v}</m> is an eigenvector of <m>T</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            We want to use that <m>\vec{v}</m> is a maximizer of <m>q_{T}</m>.
            Another way to express that condition is that moving in any direction away from <m>\vec{v}</m> gives a smaller value.
            We are going to look at each line in an orthogonal direction parametrized by a single variable, so we can use the optimality conditions from single variable calculus to argue the result.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let <m>\vec{w} \in \mathbb{S}^{d-1}</m>, and suppose that <m>\vec{w} \perp \vec{v}</m>.
            Define <m>f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{S}^{d-1}</m> by
            <me>
              f(t)=\frac{\vec{v}+t\vec{w}}{\norm{\vec{v}+t\vec{w}}}
            </me>
            Since <m>\vec{v} \perp \vec{w}</m>, by the Pythagorean theorem,
            <me>
              \|\vec{v} + t\vec{w}\|^{2} = \|\vec{v}\|^{2} + \|t\vec{w}\|^{2} = 1+t^{2}.
            </me>
            Thus,
            <me>
              f(t) = \frac{\vec{v}+t\vec{w}}{\sqrt{1+t^{2}}}.
            </me>
            Then <m>f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}^{d}</m> and <m>q_{T}: \mathbb{R}^{d} \to \mathbb{R}</m>.
            Thus, <m>q_{T}(f)</m> is a real-valued function, and we can use calculus to understand it.
          </p>

          <p>
            It turns out this single-variable function is really just a ratio of single-variable quadratic functions:
            <md>
              <mrow>q_{T}(f(t)) &amp;= \ip{\frac{\vec{v}+t\vec{w}}{\sqrt{1+t^{2}}}}{T\left(\frac{\vec{v}+t\vec{w}}{\sqrt{1+t^{2}}}\right)}</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;= \frac{\ip{\vec{v}+t\vec{w}}{T(\vec{v}+t\vec{w})}}{1+t^{2}}</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;= \frac{\ip{\vec{v}}{T\vec{v}}+ \ip{\vec{v}}{tT\vec{w}} + \ip{t\vec{w}}{T\vec{v}} + \ip{t\vec{w}}{tT\vec{w}} }{1 + t^{2}}</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;= \frac{\ip{\vec{v}}{T\vec{v}} + t(\ip{\vec{v}}{T\vec{w}} + \ip{\vec{w}}{T\vec{v}}) + t^{2} \ip{\vec{w}}{T\vec{w}}}{1+t^{2}}.</mrow>
            </md>
            The numerator of the expression at the end is quadratic.
            To simplify, I will call it <m>Q(t)</m>, and write <m>q(t) = q_{T}(f(t)) = \frac{Q(t)}{1+t^{2}}</m>.
            Now we use the quotient rule from calculus to find
            <me>
              q'(t) = \frac{Q'(t)(1+t^{2}) - Q(t)2t}{(1+t^{2})^{2}}.
            </me>
            Since <m>0</m> is a maximizer of <m>q</m> by assumption,
            <me>
              0=q'(0) = \frac{Q'(0)(1+0^{2}) - Q(0)2(0)}{(1+0^{2})^{2}} = Q'(0).
            </me>
            Note that
            <me>
              Q'(t) = \ip{\vec{v}}{T\vec{w}} + \ip{\vec{w}}{T\vec{v}} + 2t \ip{\vec{w}}{T\vec{w}}.
            </me>
            Thus,
            <me>
              0=Q'(0) = \ip{\vec{v}}{T\vec{w}} + \ip{\vec{w}}{T\vec{v}}.
            </me>
            Up to this point, the argument has not used self-adjointness.
            This is where we finally use it.
            Note that, by self-adjointness, <m>\ip{\vec{w}}{T\vec{v}} = \ip{\vec{v}}{T\vec{w}}</m>.
            Thus,
            <me>
              0 = Q'(0) = 2\ip{\vec{w}}{T\vec{v}}.
            </me>
          </p>

          <p>
            Therefore, for all <m>\vec{w}</m> orthogonal to <m>\vec{v}</m>, <m>\vec{w}</m> is orthogonal to <m>T\vec{v}</m>.
            Hence, <m>\text{span}(T\vec{v})^{\perp} \supseteq \text{span}(\vec{v})^{\perp}</m>, which implies <m>\text{span}(\vec{v}) \supseteq \text{span}(T\vec{v})</m>, and hence <m>T\vec{v}</m> is a multiple of <m>\vec{v}</m>.
            Therefore, by definition <m>\vec{v}</m> is an eigenvector of <m>T</m>.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </lemma>

      <p>
        Thus, a maximizer of <m>q_{T}</m> over <m>\mathbb{S}^{d-1}</m> must be an eigenvector, but why does this function attain its maximum?
        It turns out this requires a fact from analysis.
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-evt">
        <title>Extreme Value Theorem</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>f: \mathbb{S}^{d-1} \to \mathbb{R}</m> be a continuous function.
            Then <m>f</m> attains its maximum.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      <p>
        Applying this tells us that an eigenvector always exists.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-decomposition">
      <title>Decomposition</title>
      <p>
        The hardest part of the proof in my opinion is showing an eigenvector exists.
        For the rest, we can use the tools we developed from studying orthogonality.
      </p>

      <lemma xml:id="lem-invariant-complement">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>T</m> be a self-adjoint operator, and let <m>\vec{v}</m> be an eigenvector of <m>T</m>.
            Then <m>\text{span}(\vec{v})^\perp</m> is invariant under <m>T</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            Let <m>\vec{w}\in \text{span}(\vec{v})^\perp</m>.
            In other words, <m>\vec{w} \perp \vec{v}</m>.
            Then, writing <m>T\vec{v} = \lambda\vec{v}</m>,
            <me>
              \ip{T\vec{w}}{\vec{v}}=\ip{\vec{w}}{T\vec{v}}=\ip{\vec{w}}{\lambda \vec{v}} =0.
            </me>
            Thus <m>T\vec{w}\in \text{span}(\vec{v})^\perp</m>.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </lemma>

      <p>
        Using this insight allows us to prove the spectral theorem (<xref ref="thm-spectral"/>) by induction.
      </p>

      <proof>
        <p>
          We use induction on <m>n=\dim V</m>.
          The theorem is immediate for <m>n=1</m>.
          Assume for the sake of induction that any self-adjoint operator on an <m>n</m>-dimensional vector space has an orthonormal eigenbasis.
          We want to show it is true for an <m>(n+1)</m>-dimensional space.
          Let <m>T</m> be a self-adjoint operator on an <m>(n+1)</m>-dimensional vector space.
          Then, by the first lemma we showed today, <m>T</m> has a unit eigenvector <m>\vec{v}_1</m>.
          Its orthogonal complement <m>\text{span}(\vec{v}_1)^\perp</m> has dimension <m>n</m> and is invariant under <m>T</m> by our second lemma from today, and the restricted operator is self-adjoint.
          By induction, <m>\text{span}(\vec{v}_1)^\perp</m> has an orthonormal eigenbasis for <m>T</m>.
          Adding <m>\vec{v}_{1}</m> yields an orthonormal eigenbasis for the whole space.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </subsection>

    <exercises xml:id="sec-exercises">
      <title>Exercises</title>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-spectral-consequences">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>A</m> be a real symmetric <m>n \times n</m> matrix.
            Use the spectral theorem to prove the following:
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>A</m> has an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors;
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>Q\trans AQ=\Lambda</m> for an orthogonal <m>Q</m> and diagonal <m>\Lambda</m>;
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>A=Q\Lambda Q\trans</m>;
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>A</m> has a spectral decomposition
                  <me>
                    A=\sum_{i=1}^n\lambda_iq_iq_i\trans.
                  </me>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-circuit-walk">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Try the following <url href="https://alexblackmath.github.io/games/CircuitWalk.html">game</url>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </exercises>
  </section>
